        brokers.add(broker);
    }

    public void registerHandler(String topic, EventHandler handler) {
        handlers.put(topic, handler);
    }

    public void publish(Event event, String topic) {
        for (MessageBroker broker : brokers) {
            try {
                broker.publish(event, topic);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // Handle exceptions and fallback mechanisms
            }
        }
    }

    public Event consume(String topic) {
        for (MessageBroker broker : brokers) {
            try {
                Event event = broker.consume(topic);
                if (event != null && handlers.containsKey(topic)) {
                    handlers.get(topic).handleEvent(event);
                }
                return event;
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // Handle exceptions and retry mechanisms
            }
        }
        return null;
    }

    public void start() {
        for (MessageBroker broker : brokers) {
            new Thread(() -> {
                try {
                    while (true) {
                        Event event = consume(broker.getTopic());
                        if (event != null && handlers.containsKey(broker.getTopic())) {
                            handlers.get(broker.getTopic()).handleEvent(event);
                        }
                    }
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    // Handle exceptions and fallback mechanisms
                }
            }).start();
        }
    }
}
```

This code provides a basic structure for an event bus system in Java, including interfaces and concrete implementations for message brokers and event handlers. It also includes a plugin architecture for integrating custom message protocols. The `EventBus` class demonstrates how to register brokers and handlers and handle message publishing and consumption.

To optimize the system, further enhancements such as asynchronous processing, batching, and parallelism can be implemented. Dynamic resource allocation strategies like auto-scaling and load shedding can be added using cloud-native technologies or libraries. Error handling mechanisms, including acknowledgment mechanisms, retries, and logging, should be integrated to minimize data loss and facilitate troubleshooting. Additionally, comprehensive performance testing and monitoring should be conducted to ensure the system meets the specified operational constraints.']

