        NetworkEvent event;
        event.timestamp = time(NULL);
        event.code = EVENT_TYPE_INFO;
        snprintf(event.context.stringValue, MAX_EVENT_SIZE, "Thread %d Event", thread_id);

        pthread_mutex_lock(&buffer.lock);
        if (buffer.count < MAX_THREAD_COUNT) {
            buffer.events[buffer.count++] = event;
        }
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&buffer.lock);

        // Simulate other tasks
        sleep(1);
    }

    return NULL;
}

void init_logging() {
    pthread_mutex_init(&buffer.lock, NULL);
    buffer.count = 0;

    int thread_ids[MAX_THREAD_COUNT];
    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_THREAD_COUNT; i++) {
        thread_ids[i] = i;
        pthread_create(&thread_ids[i], NULL, log_thread, &thread_ids[i]);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_THREAD_COUNT; i++) {
        pthread_join(thread_ids[i], NULL);
    }
}

int main() {
    init_logging();
    return 0;
}
```

This code provides a basic framework for concurrent logging in C. It includes a `NetworkEvent` structure to hold log data, a `ThreadSafeBuffer` structure to safely store events from multiple threads, and a simple logging thread that simulates event generation and storage. The `init_logging` function initializes the buffer and creates several threads to simulate concurrent logging.

This example demonstrates basic concepts such as using mutexes for thread synchronization, simulating event generation, and storing data in a shared buffer. It can be expanded with additional features like error handling, memory allocation optimization, serialization/deserialization, and integration with external services as needed.']

